WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.240
Hello!

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.819
I don’t know what your last machine learning
projects have been, but look at this:

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:13.790
Researchers from Meta AI and Inria have trained
a 10 billion parameter model [meh, we’ve

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.910
seen bigger] on 1 billion Instagram images!

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.900
Wait, Ms. Coffee Bean, I know this can sound
impressive, especially comparing to the things

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:29.720
we mortals use to train, but we are already
familiar with huge models and enormous datasets.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.740
So, what is the catch of this paper?

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.580
Ah, it’s in the title!

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.640
They trained the model on an uncurated dataset
of 1 billion images and – pay attention

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.980
– even if the data is uncurated, possibly
containing the worst of what the Internet

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.230
has to offer, the model is more robust and
fair.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:58.830
This is too good to be true, I mean: we all
know the garbage in, garbage out principle:

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:04.890
How can a model trained on uncurated data
be fairer, when these models tend to reflect

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.460
and sometimes even emphasize the biases in
the data?

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.150
Well, this is what we will find out in this
AI Coffee Break!

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.799
But first, check out our sponsor of today:
Diffgram.

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:20.350
The quality of your machine learning model
depends on the quality of your training data,

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.710
making training data the heart of all Machine
Learning systems.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:31.530
But why would you trust a closed source solution
for something as important as your data?

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:37.390
Diffgram is an open-source platform for everything
related to training data.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.560
With contributors from all around the world,
Diffgram helps you create and manage unlimited

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.859
high quality training data.

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:47.560
Find out more about Diffgram from the link
in the description.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.990
Now, back to the video.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:55.409
Large self-supervised models do seem to follow
the garbage in garbage out principle.

00:01:55.409 --> 00:02:00.469
Just think about GPT-3 that was trained on
large datasets scraped from the internet.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:07.270
It produces toxic output, false information,
conspiracy theories, it exhibits sexism and

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.270
racism.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:13.470
But Ms. Coffee Bean, today we are not concerned
with text models, but with large self-supervised

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:14.890
vision models.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:20.440
On the vision realm, the toxicity meter is
high too, because large vision models trained

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.190
on ImageNet, reflect the biases in the data.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:29.740
And yes, the one and only ImageNet contains
problematic content, is biased by covering

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:34.860
some geographical locations more than others,
some races more than others.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.880
So, no wonder that vision models reflect these
biases too.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.390
What to do to fix this toxic model behaviour?

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.640
What people did so far, especially for ImageNet,
was: curate the dataset.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:54.450
Also, for text, OpenAI released a better version
of GPT-3, called InstructGPT, by fine-tuning

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.410
GPT-3 on a curated dataset.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:03.220
Ok, so curating datasets is one solution,
but you can imagine how laborious and costly

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.510
it can be to annotate datasets!

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:12.650
The larger the model, the larger the datasets
and dataset curation with little curated data

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.880
can only get you so far.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.310
So, what to do to create fairer models if
dataset curation is so expensive?

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:25.870
Well, the authors of this paper say: Okay,
just ditch dataset curation altogether and

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:31.980
train the model on an uncurated dataset and
still get a model that is fair, even fairer

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.390
than previous models!

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.340
Wow, I wonder why nobody had this idea earlier.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.560
Or they did, but they ended up with unfair
models.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.000
Now we are really curious to find out how
the authors managed to train on potentially

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.790
garbage data and get a shiny model out of
it.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.870
We are not sarcastic, really, so let’s follow
the paper to see.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:59.770
The idea of the paper is not new: It relies
on the experimental evidence that the more

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.890
data, the better the features the neural network
can learn.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:06.630
This is what the developments over the last
years have shown us.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:12.350
Okay then, the authors took 1 billion random
public images from Instagram.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:20.280
The only criterion was that the images should
come from non-EU countries to comply to GDPR.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:27.740
Okay, now the Meta AI researchers sit with
these 1 billion images, what to do with them?

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:28.740
Curate them?

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.009
Filter them?

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:33.199
Nah, the dataset is too big for that.

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:38.260
And we told you, they decided to ditch dataset
curation and still hope for fairness (yeah,

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.260
but how)?

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.560
Anyway, the authors trained a model and called
it SEER

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.100
which is just a RegNet architecture.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:51.790
A RegNet is a variant of the well-known ResNet
where the residual connections are regulated

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.580
by a recurrent neural network (RNN).

00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:01.169
The previous biggest model in the RegNet family
was only 1.5 billion parameters big.

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:06.380
In order not to underfit the data, the model
size should match or surpass the dataset size,

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:14.409
so the authors scaled up the RegNet to make
SEER, a RegNet model scaled up to 10 billion

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:15.409
parameters.

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:21.080
Just imagine the necessary engineering work
to parallelize this thing on a cluster.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.400
But you do not need to imagine the work, you
can read a lot of details in the paper about

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:32.669
how they pre-trained the model on 496 NVIDIA
A100 GPUs.

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:36.279
(I do not have these at home, do you?)

00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:40.930
This paper covers the hardware aspect far
more extensively than usual papers.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.960
But now let’s discuss how the authors trained
this thing.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.710
SEER was pretrained with SwAV self-supervision.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:54.240
This basically teaches the model to compute
representations of the data such that the

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.050
representations of different patches of the
same image are assigned to the same cluster.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:05.830
And then for using and testing SEER on interesting
downstream tasks, the authors appended a linear

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.949
layer and fine-tuned the model for each task.

00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:14.909
So, in terms of architecture and self-supervised
training objective, this paper does not bring

00:06:14.909 --> 00:06:16.499
anything new.

00:06:16.499 --> 00:06:20.999
The paper really shines when it comes to evaluating
the model.

00:06:20.999 --> 00:06:25.009
The authors evaluate SEER on 50 benchmarks
in total!

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:30.889
And these tests contain classical computer
vision tasks like image classification or

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:36.190
detecting whether an image is a copy of another,
which is useful for enforcing copyright.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:42.030
Of course, the large model trained on huge
amounts of data has good visual representations

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:47.909
and is either keeping pace with the competition
or establishes new state-of-the-art.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:52.990
But this is not what we are concerned with
today, because within those 50 benchmarks,

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:56.460
there are also 4 fairness benchmarks.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:02.120
To summarize very long experimental sections,
the authors basically compare a SEER model

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:08.249
trained on the 1 billion uncurated Instagram
images against a smaller model version trained

00:07:08.249 --> 00:07:09.599
on ImageNet.

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:14.699
Consistently, SEER is fairer when it comes
to metrics measuring potential biases with

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:19.419
respect to skin-color, age groups, gender,
geographical diversity.

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:23.539
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean, but HOW does this happen?

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:29.490
It is totally expected that SEER, being a
big model having seen lots of training data,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:34.710
can impress on computer vision tasks, having
learned good visual features.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.610
But why is it fairer?

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:44.879
It is trained on uncurated data, as we are
reminded by the title, the abstract,

00:07:44.879 --> 00:07:47.349
and the introduction of the paper.

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:52.110
Why does SEER not reflect biases as we have
seen with GPT-3?

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:56.819
Well, the explanation of the authors is that
the model, having seen lots and lots of data,

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:03.810
is not only capable of capturing better features,
but other human values too.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.439
And this was it, the explanation.

00:08:07.439 --> 00:08:11.360
We could not find further information about
this in the paper.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.930
Or maybe, the paper just does not say “Instagram
images” as often as it should but chooses

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.800
to say “random internet images”.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:27.360
The word Instagram first occurs in the paper
in the caption of Figure 2

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.330
and Section 3.2 in the main text body!

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:38.370
So, SEER is not trained on any uncurated dataset,
but on Instagram images.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:43.919
Instagram pictures are subject to the platform’s
policy and upload guidelines.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:48.101
These counter hate speech, targeting individuals,
and harassment.

00:08:48.101 --> 00:08:54.411
So sure, the authors do not curate the 1 billion
Instagram images used for training SEER, but

00:08:54.411 --> 00:09:00.130
Instagram’s guidelines themselves acted
as pre-filtering and data curation to reflect

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:05.329
a, we cite “diverse community of cultures,
ages and beliefs”.

00:09:05.329 --> 00:09:10.709
Then the lesson of the paper and the SEER
model is sure, Instagram images might not

00:09:10.709 --> 00:09:17.480
need more filtering and curation,
but not any uncurated image dataset can provide

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.910
a fairness basis for large vision models.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:25.500
This message can be still seen clearly when
carefully reading the paper, but we still

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.050
find the title, abstract and introduction
not transparent enough on this.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:35.399
Especially, we would have liked to see experiments
that assess how certain things influence the

00:09:35.399 --> 00:09:42.990
quality of the dataset, such as the upload
guidelines and policies of Instagram, the

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:49.199
correlation between Instagram usage and the
ethical values of the users, and so on.

00:09:49.199 --> 00:09:55.009
So, with this paper, we have seen again that
scaling laws still apply, and data and model

00:09:55.009 --> 00:10:02.959
sizes can get you good downstream task performance
and maybe, but only maybe other emergent phenomena,

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:03.959
such as fairness.

00:10:03.959 --> 00:10:09.480
We would still be very cautious to say that
uncurated data is the way to go.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.269
We consider uncurated data in general to be
still unsafe and we should not just wait and

00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:24.639
pray for models trained on uncurated datasets
to avoid biases and toxic behavior.

00:10:24.639 --> 00:10:27.949
We should make an active effort about this.

00:10:27.949 --> 00:10:28.949
What do you think?

00:10:28.949 --> 00:10:32.759
Are you now an uncurated dataset believer?

00:10:32.759 --> 00:10:33.860
Let us know in the comments!

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:34.049
Okay, bye!

